Z||000000||FieroBUZZ||10-10-2001||07:49 PM||||We've all seen and admired some of the cool things dome to our cars. From little touches like a drink holder to some of Aus' wild ideas.<BR>How about everyone do a short how-to on their one fav mod and post it here at the forum or elsewhere.  Either something they've done or a possible (do-able) mod with photoshop or whatever.<BR>Get those creative juices flowing!!!<BR>Ideas or suggestions welcome!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||10-11-2001||01:06 AM||||How about I start this out with a mod every single Fiero should have...<P>Manual radiator fan switch!<P>First get a simple on/off switch from Radio Shack or someplace similar, I got one rated for 5 amps or something like that.  Don't think amp rating really matters since all you're gonna be doing with it is grounding the fan switch, but I wanted to be on the safe side.  Get a few feet of wire.  That's about it for the materials portion.<P>Then, crawl under your dashboard on the drivers side.  You'll see some big connectors with a bunch of wires going into the front compartment, we're interested in the one closest to the center console (farthest left, if you're laying on the floor looking up).  Specifically, the third wire from the bottom on the outermost-left row of wires, should be green with a white stripe.  Now, splice the length of wire you have into the green/white wire.  <B>Don't cut it</B>!!  Run the wire to where you want the switch to be (I have mine in the driver's ashtray) and cut it.  Attach it to one pole on the switch.  Now it's time to find your ground point.<P>Now's a good time to take out the center console and shift surround, very easy task if you haven't done it before.  Take off your shift knob (unscrew for sticks, c-clip in front for auto's) and take out the ashtray's.  You'll see 4 7mm bolt under the ashtrays.  Take those out.  Take out the cig. lighter trim plate and the cig. lighter.  Two 7mm bolts at the front of the console and two behind the glove compartment hold it in.  Take out those and lift it up out of the car.<P>Now, look on the top of the metal tunnel towards the back of the car, on the passenger side, and you'll see a ground point for the ecm(?) there.  This is where I grounded my switch (and my amp, when I had it installed).  You can either get fancy and use a spade terminal for the wire, or just ghetto it like me and strip 1/2" of wire and wrap it around the screw  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Again, run the wire out to where you want the switch, trim, and connect to the other pole on the switch.  Best bet is to solder the wires on to the switch, but I just went ghetto-style again and didn't.  <P>Now, before you put everything back together, test it out!  Turn the key to "on" (engine doesn't have to be running) and, if the fan turns on as soon as you do that you just found out which position is on for the switch  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Otherwise, flip the switch and listen for the fan.  If you don't hear it, make sure you're getting full contact at the splice, ground, and the switch.  Not much  to go wrong here.<P>Now it's time to bolt everything back up!  Also a good oppertunity to clean up underneath the shift console, lot's of crud likes to collect under there.  <P>I've had this setup on my GT for a year and a half or so, and it was one of the first things I did to the quad coupe when I got it home.  One of the best things you can do, IMHO.  Have fun!<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">         <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||pontiacs4me||10-11-2001||01:11 AM||||Standard, I grounded mine perminately so the fan runs all the time, is this bad?  I sometimes wonder if my car runs too cool.  It seems to be going into closed loop, because I get pretty good mileage.  What do you think?<P>Oh yeah, about mods, L67 3800 series II supercharged V6, that is the mod of all mods.  I picked up mine today from a local salvage, with wiring, and matching transmission and etc for $1694.40.<P>------------------<BR>Soon to be: L67 powered 87 coupe.||||reg||1
